ultiplying each part separately before adding the results.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 72 can be broken down into 70 and 2.\n   - 34 can be broken down into 30 and 4.\n\n2. **Multiply each part**:\n   - Multiply 70 by 30:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 30 = 2100\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 70 by 4:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 4 = 280\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 2 by 30:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 30 = 60\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 2 by 4:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 4 = 8\n     \\]\n\n3. **Add the partial products**:\n   \\[\n   2100 + 280 + 60 + 8 = 2448\n   \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 72 and 34 is \\(2448\\).\n\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nUsing the partial products method, we solve 72 \u00d7 34 as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 72 can be broken down into 70 and 2.\n   - 34 can be broken down into 30 and 4.\n\n2. **Multiply each part**:\n   - Multiply 70 by 30:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 30 = 2100\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 70 by 4:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 4 = 280\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 2 by 30:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 30 = 60\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 2 by 4:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 4 = 8\n     \\]\n\n3. **Add the partial products**:\n   \\[\n   2100 + 280 + 60 + 8 = 2448\n   \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 72 and 34 is \\(2448\\).\n\n</answer>.<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the partial products method. It's very clear.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>.\nYou're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation using the partial products method clear. If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Understanding different methods for multiplication can help you choose the most efficient approach for any given problem. <|User|>Could you provide an example of how to solve this using the area model with a different number, say 65 \u00d7 42?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve 65 \u00d7 42 using the area model. The area model breaks down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts by visualizing the numbers as rectangles.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 65 can be broken down into 60 and 5.\n   - 42 can be broken down into 40 and 2.\n\n2. **Set up the area model**:\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles.\n   - Label the sides of the rectangle with the place values of the numbers: 60 and 5 on one side, and 40 and 2 on the other side.\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 60 and 40:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 40 = 2400\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 60 and 2:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 2 = 120\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 5 and 40:\n     \\[\n     5 \\times 40 = 200\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 5 and 2:\n     \\[\n     5 \\times 2 = 10\n     \\]\n\n4. **Add the areas of the smaller rectangles**:\n   \\[\n   2400 + 120 + 200 + 10 = 2730\n   \\]\n\nSo, using the area model, the product of 65 and 42 is \\(2730\\).\n\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nUsing the area model, we solve 65 \u00d7 42 as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 65 can be broken down into 60 and 5.\n   - 42 can be broken down into 40 and 2.\n\n2. **Set up the area model**:\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles.\n   - Label the sides of the rectangle with the place values of the numbers: 60 and 5 on one side, and 40 and 2 on the other side.\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 60 and 40:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 40 = 2400\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 60 and 2:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 2 = 120\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 5 and 40:\n     \\[\n     5 \\times 40 = 200\n     \\]\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 5 and 2:\n 