.\n   - New number of months (\\(m_2\\)): 6 (0.5 years).\n\n2. **Calculate the amount after the first 15 years:**.\n   - Monthly interest rate (\\(r_{m1}\\)): \\(\\frac{0.00025}{12} = 0.00002083\\).\n   - Amount after 15 years:.\n     \\[.\n     A_1 = P \\times (1 + r_{m1})^{m_1} = 10000 \\times (1 + 0.00002083)^{180} \\approx 10000 \\times 1.0030 = 10030.00.\n     \\].\n\n3. **Calculate the amount after the remaining 0.5 years:**.\n   - Monthly interest rate (\\(r_{m2}\\)): \\(\\frac{0.001}{12} = 0.0000833\\).\n   - Amount after 15.5 years:.\n     \\[.\n     A_2 = A_1 \\times (1 + r_{m2})^{m_2} = 10030.00 \\times (1 + 0.0000833)^{6} \\approx 10030.00 \\times 1.0025 = 10052.56.\n     \\].\n\n### Using the Distributive Property:.\nWe can use the distributive property conceptually to understand the breakdown of the loan amount over the different periods. The loan amount can be thought of as the sum of the principal amount and the interest for each period, which can be distributed over the remaining balance.\n\n- **Amount after the first 15 years:**.\n  - Principal portion: $10,000.\n  - Interest portion: \\(10,000 \\times 0.00025 \\times 15 = 37.5\\).\n  - Total amount after 15 years: \\(10,000 + 37.5 = 10,037.5\\).\n\n- **Amount after the remaining 0.5 years:**.\n  - Principal portion: $10,037.5.\n  - Interest portion: \\(10,037.5 \\times 0.001 \\times 0.5 = 5.01875\\).\n  - Total amount after 15.5 years: \\(10,037.5 + 5.01875 = 10,042.51875\\).\n\nBy using the distributive property conceptually, we can see that the total amount after 15.5 years is the sum of the principal amount and the interest for each period, which simplifies the calculation of the total cost of the loan.\n\nThis example demonstrates how the distributive property can be used to understand the breakdown of a loan with varying interest rates over different periods, making it easier to manage and understand the total cost of the loan. <|User|>This is really helpful. Can you provide another example o