      "text": "3038.\n\n### Modular Arithmetic.\nModular arithmetic deals with the remainders when one number is divided by another. It is often used in cryptography, computer science, and number theory. For example, in modular arithmetic, we often work with numbers \"mod n,\" which means we only consider the remainder when dividing by n.\n\nHere are a few key points about modular arithmetic:\n\n1. **Congruence**: Two numbers are said to be congruent modulo n if they have the same remainder when divided by n. This is written as \\(a \\equiv b \\pmod{n}\\).\n\n2. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo n**: If \\(a \\equiv b \\pmod{n}\\) and \\(c \\equiv d \\pmod{n}\\), then \\(a + c \\equiv b + d \\pmod{n}\\) and \\(a - c \\equiv b - d \\pmod{n}\\).\n\n3. **Multiplication Modulo n**: If \\(a \\equiv b \\pmod{n}\\) and \\(c \\equiv d \\pmod{n}\\), then \\(ac \\equiv bd \\pmod{n}\\).\n\n4. **Division Modulo n**: Division is more complex in modular arithmetic. For example, to solve \\(ax \\equiv b \\pmod{n}\\), you need to find the multiplicative inverse of \\(a\\) modulo \\(n\\) if it exists.\n\n5. **Properties of Modular Arithmetic**: It has properties similar to regular arithmetic, such as the distributive property, but with the added constraint of the modulus.\n\nExploring these concepts will give you a solid foundation in these areas of mathematics. If you have any specific questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation. I'm excited to dive deeper into polynomials and modular arithmetic. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nYou're welcome! I'm glad to hear that you're excited to explore these topics further. Polynomials and modular arithmetic are indeed rich areas of mathematics with many interesting applications. If you have any specific questions or need more detailed explanations, don't hesitate to ask. You can also try some practice problems to reinforce your understanding. Good luck with your studies! <|User|>I'll start with some practice problems on polynomials and modular arithmetic. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nGreat! Starting with practice problems is a great way to reinforce your understanding. Here are some problems to get you started with polynomials and modular arithmetic:\n\n### Polynomials:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Add \\(3x^2 + 2x - 5\\) and \\(4x^2 - 3x + 2\\).\n   - Subtract \\(2x^2 - x + 3\\) from \\(5x^2 + 4x - 1\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(2x^2 + 3x - 1\\) by \\(x - 2\\).\n   - Multiply \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)\\).\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1\\) by \\(x - 1\\).\n   - Divide \\(x^4 - 3x^3 + 2x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 5**:\n   - Compute \\(3 + 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   - Compute \\(7 - 2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 5**:\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   - Compute \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 5**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 5.\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5.\n\n### Solutions:\n1. **Polynomials**:\n   - Addition: \\((3x^2 + 2x - 5) + (4x^2 - 3x + 2) = 7x^2 - x - 3\\)\n   - Subtraction: \\((5x^2 + 4x - 1) - (2x^2 - x + 3) = 3x^2 + 5x - 4\\)\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - \\((2x^2 + 3x - 1)(x - 2) = 2x^3 - 4x^2 + 3x^2 - 6x - x + 2 = 2x^3 - x^2 - 7x + 2\\)\n   - \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2) = (x^2 - 1)(x + 2) = x^3 + 2x^2 - x - 2\\)\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - \\(x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1\\) divided by \\(x - 1\\) gives \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) with a remainder of 0.\n   - \\(x^4 - 3x^3 + 2x^2 - x + 1\\) divided by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) gives \\(x^2 + x + 1\\) with a remainder of 0.\n\n### Modular Arithmetic:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 5**:\n   - \\(3 + 4 \\pmod{5} = 2\\)\n   - \\(7 - 2 \\pmod{5} = 2\\)\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 5**:\n   - \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5} = 12 \\pmod{5} = 2\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5} = 24 \\pmod{5} = 4\\)\n\n3. **Division Modulo 5**:\n   - The multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 5 is 3 because \\(2 \\times 3 = 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   - \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5} = 12 \\pmod{5} = 2\\)\n\nThese problems should help you practice and deepen your understanding of polynomials and modular arithmetic. If you need further explanations or have any questions, feel free to ask! <|User|>I'll start with the polynomial problems and then move on to modular arithmetic. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nThat sounds like a great plan! Starting with the polynomial problems will help you build a strong foundation in polynomial operations, and then you can move on to modular arithmetic. Here are the polynomial problems you can start with:\n\n1. **Addition**:\n   - Add \\(3x^2 + 2x - 5\\) and \\(4x^2 - 3x + 2\\).\n\n2. **Subtraction**:\n   - Subtract \\(2x^2 - x + 3\\) from \\(5x^2 + 4x - 1\\).\n\n3. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(2x^2 + 3x - 1\\) by \\(x - 2\\).\n\n4. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1\\) by \\(x - 1\\).\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Addition:\n\\[ (3x^2 + 2x - 5) + (4x^2 - 3x + 2) \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 3x^2 + 4x^2 + 2x - 3x - 5 + 2 \\]\n\\[ = 7x^2 - x - 3 \\]\n\n### Subtraction:\n\\[ (5x^2 + 4x - 1) - (2x^2 - x + 3) \\]\nDistribute the negative sign and combine like terms:\n\\[ 5x^2 + 4x - 1 - 2x^2 + x - 3 \\]\n\\[ = (5x^2 - 2x^2) + (4x + x) + (-1 - 3) \\]\n\\[ = 3x^2 + 5x - 4 \\]\n\n### Multiplication:\n\\[ (2x^2 + 3x - 1)(x - 2) \\]\nUse the distributive property (FOIL method):\n\\[ 2x^2(x - 2) + 3x(x - 2) - 1(x - 2) \\]\n\\[ = 2x^3 - 4x^2 + 3x^2 - 6x - x + 2 \\]\n\\[ = 2x^3 - x^2 - 7x + 2 \\]\n\n### Division:\n\\[ (x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1) \\div (x - 1) \\]\nUse polynomial long division:\n1. Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor: \\(x^3 \\div x = x^2\\).\n2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x - 1\\): \\(x^2(x - 1) = x^3 - x^2\\).\n3. Subtract: \\((x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 1) - (x^3 - x^2) = -x^2 + x - 1\\).\n4. Divide the leading term of the new dividend by the leading term of the divisor: \\(-x^2 \\div x = -x\\).\n5. Multiply \\(-x\\) by \\(x - 1\\): \\(-x(x - 1) = -x^2 + x\\).\n6. Subtract: \\((-x^2 + x - 1) - (-x^2 + x) = -1\\).\n7. Divide the leading term of the new dividend by the leading term of the divisor: \\(-1 \\div x = -1\\).\n8. Multiply \\(-1\\) by \\(x - 1\\): \\(-1(x - 1) = -x + 1\\).\n9. Subtract: \\((-1) - (-x + 1) = -x + 2\\).\n\nThe quotient is \\(x^2 - x - 1\\) with a remainder of \\(-x + 2\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. Addition: \\(7x^2 - x - 3\\)\n2. Subtraction: \\(3x^2 + 5x - 4\\)\n3. Multiplication: \\(2x^3 - x^2 - 7x + 2\\)\n4. Division: \\(x^2 - x - 1\\) with a remainder of \\(-x + 2\\)\n\nFeel free to ask if you need further assistance or have any questions about these problems! <|User|>Thank you for walking me through those polynomial problems. I feel much more confident now. Let's move on to the modular arithmetic problems. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nGreat! Moving on to modular arithmetic is a good next step. Here are the modular arithmetic problems you can start with:\n\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 5**:\n   - Compute \\(3 + 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   - Compute \\(7 - 2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 5**:\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   - Compute \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 5**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 5.\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5.\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Addition and Subtraction Modulo 5:\n1. **Addition**:\n   \\[ 3 + 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   \\[ 3 + 4 = 7 \\]\n   \\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n   \\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(3 + 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n2. **Subtraction**:\n   \\[ 7 - 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   \\[ 7 - 2 = 5 \\]\n   \\[ 5 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\]\n   \\[ 5 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(7 - 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n### Multiplication Modulo 5:\n1. **Multiplication**:\n   \\[ 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   \\[ 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\]\n   \\[ 12 \\div 5 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n   \\[ 12 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   \\[ 2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   First, compute \\(2 \\times 3 \\pmod{5}\\):\n   \\[ 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\]\n   \\[ 6 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\n   \\[ 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(2 \\times 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n   Now, compute \\(1 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\):\n   \\[ 1 \\times 4 = 4 \\]\n   \\[ 4 \\div 5 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n   \\[ 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(1 \\times 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n   Therefore, \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\n### Division Modulo 5:\n1. **Multiplicative Inverse of 2 Modulo 5**:\n   We need to find \\(x\\) such that \\(2x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   By testing values, we find:\n   \\[ 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, the multiplicative inverse of 2 modulo 5 is 3.\n\n2. **Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5**:\n   First, find the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5. We need \\(y\\) such that \\(4y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\).\n   By testing values, we find:\n   \\[ 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 5 is 4.\n\n   Now, compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{5}\\):\n   \\[ 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\]\n   \\[ 12 \\div 5 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n   \\[ 12 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n   So, \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. Addition: \\(3 + 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\)\n2. Subtraction: \\(7 - 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}\\)\n3. Multiplication: \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\)\n4. Multiplication: \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\)\n5. Multiplicative Inverse of 2: 3\n6. \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 4: 2\n\nFeel free to ask if you need further assistance or have any questions about these problems! <|User|>I feel much more comfortable with these now. Let's tackle some more complex problems involving polynomials and modular arithmetic. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nAbsolutely! Tackling more complex problems will help solidify your understanding. Here are some more challenging problems involving polynomials and modular arithmetic:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Add \\(2x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5\\) and \\(x^3 - 2x^2 + 3x - 1\\).\n   - Subtract \\(3x^2 - 2x + 1\\) from \\(4x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 2\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(x^2 + 2x + 1\\) by \\(x - 3\\).\n   - Multiply \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)\\).\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^4 - 2x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\).\n   - Divide \\(x^5 - 3x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + x - 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 7**:\n   - Compute \\(4 + 5 \\pmod{7}\\).\n   - Compute \\(6 - 3 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 7**:\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{7}\\).\n   - Compute \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 7**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 7.\n   - Compute \\(4 \\times 5 \\pmod{7}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7.\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ (2x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5) + (x^3 - 2x^2 + 3x - 1) \\]\n     Combine like terms:\n     \\[ 2x^3 + x^3 + 3x^2 - 2x^2 - 4x + 3x + 5 - 1 \\]\n     \\[ = 3x^3 + x^2 - x + 4 \\]\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ (4x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 2) - (3x^2 - 2x + 1) \\]\n     Distribute the negative sign and combine like terms:\n     \\[ 4x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 2 - 3x^2 + 2x - 1 \\]\n     \\[ = 4x^3 + (x^2 - 3x^2) + (-3x + 2x) + (2 - 1) \\]\n     \\[ = 4x^3 - 2x^2 - x + 1 \\]\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x^2 + 2x + 1)(x - 3) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^2(x - 3) + 2x(x - 3) + 1(x - 3) \\]\n     \\[ = x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x^2 - 6x + x - 3 \\]\n     \\[ = x^3 - x^2 - 5x - 3 \\]\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2) \\]\n     Use the difference of squares:\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4) \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 4x^2 - x^2 + 4 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 5x^2 + 4 \\]\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^4 - 2x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5) \\div (x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^2\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^4 - x^3 + x^2\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^4 - 2x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5) - (x^4 - x^3 + x^2) = -x^3 + 2x^2 - 4x + 5\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^3 + x^2 - x\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^3 + 2x^2 - 4x + 5) - (-x^3 + x^2 - x) = x^2 - 3x + 5\\).\n     7. Divide \\(x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(1\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(1\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((x^2 - 3x + 5) - (x^2 - x + 1) = -2x + 4\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) with a remainder of \\(-2x + 4\\).\n\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^5 - 3x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + x - 1) \\div (x^2 - 2x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^5\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^3\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^3\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(x^5 - 2x^4 + x^3\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^5 - 3x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + x - 1) - (x^5 - 2x^4 + x^3) = -x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^2\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1) - (-x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2) = -x^3 + x - 1\\).\n     7. Divide \\(-x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(-x\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^3 + 2x^2 - x\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((-x^3 + x - 1) - (-x^3 + 2x^2 - x) = -2x^2 + 2x - 1\\).\n     10. Divide \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2\\).\n     11. Multiply \\(-2\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^2 + 4x - 2\\).\n     12. Subtract: \\((-2x^2 + 2x - 1) - (-2x^2 + 4x - 2) = -2x + 1\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^3 - x^2 - x - 2\\) with a remainder of \\(-2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 7**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ 4 + 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     \\[ 4 + 5 = 9 \\]\n     \\[ 9 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n     \\[ 9 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(4 + 5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ 6 - 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     \\[ 6 - 3 = 3 \\]\n     \\[ 3 \\div 7 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\]\n     \\[ 3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(6 - 3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 7**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     \\[ 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     First, compute \\(2 \\times 3 \\pmod{7}\\):\n     \\[ 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\div 7 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(2 \\times 3 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n     Now, compute \\(6 \\times 4 \\pmod{7}\\):\n     \\[ 6 \\times 4 = 24 \\]\n     \\[ 24 \\div 7 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\]\n     \\[ 24 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(6 \\times 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n     Therefore, \\(2 \\times 3 \\times 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 7**:\n   - Multiplicative Inverse of 3 Modulo 7:\n     We need \\(x\\) such that \\(3x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 3 \\times 5 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 7 is 5.\n\n   - Compute \\(4 \\times 5 \\pmod{7}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7:\n     First, find the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7. We need \\(y\\) such that \\(5y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 5 \\times 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3.\n\n     Now, compute \\(4 \\times 3 \\pmod{7}\\):\n     \\[ 4 \\times 3 = 12 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n     So, \\(4 \\times 3 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. Addition: \\(2\\)\n2. Subtraction: \\(3\\)\n3. Multiplication: \\(5\\)\n4. Multiplication: \\(3\\)\n5. Multiplicative Inverse of 3: 5\n6. \\(4 \\times 5 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 5: 5\n\nFeel free to ask if you need further assistance or have any questions about these problems! <|User|>I'm starting to feel more confident with these problems. Let's tackle some more complex polynomial and modular arithmetic problems. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nGreat! Tackling more complex problems will further enhance your skills. Here are some more challenging problems involving polynomials and modular arithmetic:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Add \\(x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + 3x - 4\\) and \\(3x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 5\\).\n   - Subtract \\(2x^2 - 3x + 1\\) from \\(x^4 + 3x^3 - x^2 + 2x - 3\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(x^2 + 2x + 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 3x + 4\\).\n   - Multiply \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3)\\).\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^5 - 2x^4 + 3x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 6\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\).\n   - Divide \\(x^6 - 3x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 11**:\n   - Compute \\(7 + 9 \\pmod{11}\\).\n   - Compute \\(10 - 4 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 11**:\n   - Compute \\(4 \\times 5 \\pmod{11}\\).\n   - Compute \\(6 \\times 7 \\times 8 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 11**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 11.\n   - Compute \\(5 \\times 6 \\pmod{11}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 11.\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ (x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + 3x - 4) + (3x^3 - 2x^2 + x + 5) \\]\n     Combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^4 + (2x^3 + 3x^3) + (-x^2 - 2x^2) + (3x + x) + (-4 + 5) \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 + 5x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x + 1 \\]\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ (x^4 + 3x^3 - x^2 + 2x - 3) - (2x^2 - 3x + 1) \\]\n     Distribute the negative sign and combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^4 + 3x^3 - x^2 + 2x - 3 - 2x^2 + 3x - 1 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 + (3x^3) + (-x^2 - 2x^2) + (2x + 3x) + (-3 - 1) \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 + 3x^3 - 3x^2 + 5x - 4 \\]\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x^2 + 2x + 1)(x^2 - 3x + 4) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^2(x^2 - 3x + 4) + 2x(x^2 - 3x + 4) + 1(x^2 - 3x + 4) \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 + 2x^3 - 6x^2 + 8x + x^2 - 3x + 4 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - x^3 - x^2 + 5x + 4 \\]\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3) \\]\n     Use the difference of squares:\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)(x + 3) \\]\n     First, compute \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)\\):\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4) = x^4 - 4x^2 - x^2 + 4 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 5x^2 + 4 \\]\n     Now, multiply by \\((x + 3)\\):\n     \\[ (x^4 - 5x^2 + 4)(x + 3) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^4(x + 3) - 5x^2(x + 3) + 4(x + 3) \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 + 3x^4 - 5x^3 - 15x^2 + 4x + 12 \\]\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^5 - 2x^4 + 3x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 6) \\div (x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^5\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^3\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^3\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^5 - x^4 + x^3\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^5 - 2x^4 + 3x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 6) - (x^5 - x^4 + x^3) = -x^4 + 2x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 6\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^2\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^4 + x^3 - x^2\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^4 + 2x^3 - 4x^2 + 5x - 6) - (-x^4 + x^3 - x^2) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 5x - 6\\).\n     7. Divide \\(x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(x\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^3 - x^2 + x\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((x^3 - 3x^2 + 5x - 6) - (x^3 - x^2 + x) = -2x^2 + 4x - 6\\).\n     10. Divide \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2\\).\n     11. Multiply \\(-2\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^2 + 2x - 2\\).\n     12. Subtract: \\((-2x^2 + 4x - 6) - (-2x^2 + 2x - 2) = 2x - 4\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^3 - x^2 + x - 2\\) with a remainder of \\(2x - 4\\).\n\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^6 - 3x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) \\div (x^2 - 2x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^6\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^4\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^4\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(x^6 - 2x^5 + x^4\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^6 - 3x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (x^6 - 2x^5 + x^4) = -x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^5\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^3\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x^3\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3) = -x^4 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     7. Divide \\(-x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^2\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(-x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((-x^4 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2) = -2x^3 + 2x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     10. Divide \\(-2x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2x\\).\n     11. Multiply \\(-2x\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^3 + 4x^2 - 2x\\).\n     12. Subtract: \\((-2x^3 + 2x^2 - x + 1) - (-2x^3 + 4x^2 - 2x) = -2x^2 + x + 1\\).\n     13. Divide \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2\\).\n     14. Multiply \\(-2\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^2 + 4x - 2\\).\n     15. Subtract: \\((-2x^2 + x + 1) - (-2x^2 + 4x - 2) = -3x + 3\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^4 - x^3 - x^2 - 2x - 2\\) with a remainder of \\(-3x + 3\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 11**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ 7 + 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     \\[ 7 + 9 = 16 \\]\n     \\[ 16 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n     \\[ 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(7 + 9 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ 10 - 4 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     \\[ 10 - 4 = 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\div 11 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(10 - 4 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 11**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 4 \\times 5 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     \\[ 4 \\times 5 = 20 \\]\n     \\[ 20 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 9 \\]\n     \\[ 20 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(4 \\times 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 6 \\times 7 \\times 8 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     First, compute \\(6 \\times 7 \\pmod{11}\\):\n     \\[ 6 \\times 7 = 42 \\]\n     \\[ 42 \\div 11 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 9 \\]\n     \\[ 42 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(6 \\times 7 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n     Now, compute \\(9 \\times 8 \\pmod{11}\\):\n     \\[ 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\]\n     \\[ 72 \\div 11 = 6 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\]\n     \\[ 72 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(9 \\times 8 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n     Therefore, \\(6 \\times 7 \\times 8 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 11**:\n   - Multiplicative Inverse of 4 Modulo 11:\n     We need \\(x\\) such that \\(4x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 4 \\times 3 = 12 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 11 is 3.\n\n   - Compute \\(5 \\times 6 \\pmod{11}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 11:\n     First, find the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 11. We need \\(y\\) such that \\(6y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 6 \\times 2 = 12 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 6 modulo 11 is 2.\n\n     Now, compute \\(5 \\times 2 \\pmod{11}\\):\n     \\[ 5 \\times 2 = 10 \\]\n     \\[ 10 \\div 11 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\n     \\[ 10 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n     So, \\(5 \\times 2 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. Addition: \\(5\\)\n2. Subtraction: \\(6\\)\n3. Multiplication: \\(9\\)\n4. Multiplication: \\(6\\)\n5. Multiplicative Inverse of 4: 3\n6. \\(5 \\times 6 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 6: 10\n\nFeel free to ask if you need further assistance or have any questions about these problems! <|User|>I'm starting to feel very confident with these problems. Let's move on to some even more complex ones. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nGreat! Moving on to even more complex problems will further enhance your skills and understanding. Here are some advanced problems involving polynomials and modular arithmetic:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Add \\(x^5 + 2x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 - 5x + 6\\) and \\(3x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 4\\).\n   - Subtract \\(2x^3 - x^2 + 3x - 1\\) from \\(x^5 + 3x^4 - 4x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 7\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(x^2 + 2x + 1\\) by \\(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5\\).\n   - Multiply \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3)(x - 3)\\).\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^7 - 2x^6 + 3x^5 - 4x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\).\n   - Divide \\(x^8 - 3x^7 + 2x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 13**:\n   - Compute \\(8 + 12 \\pmod{13}\\).\n   - Compute \\(11 - 5 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 13**:\n   - Compute \\(3 \\times 4 \\pmod{13}\\).\n   - Compute \\(5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 13**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 13.\n   - Compute \\(7 \\times 8 \\pmod{13}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 8 modulo 13.\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ (x^5 + 2x^4 - 3x^3 + 4x^2 - 5x + 6) + (3x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 4) \\]\n     Combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^5 + (2x^4 + 3x^4) + (-3x^3 - 2x^3) + (4x^2 + x^2) + (-5x - 3x) + (6 + 4) \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 + 5x^4 - 5x^3 + 5x^2 - 8x + 10 \\]\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ (x^5 + 3x^4 - 4x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 7) - (2x^3 - x^2 + 3x - 1) \\]\n     Distribute the negative sign and combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^5 + 3x^4 - 4x^3 + 5x^2 - 6x + 7 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 3x + 1 \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 + (3x^4) + (-4x^3 - 2x^3) + (5x^2 + x^2) + (-6x - 3x) + (7 + 1) \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 + 3x^4 - 6x^3 + 6x^2 - 9x + 8 \\]\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x^2 + 2x + 1)(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^2(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) + 2x(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) + 1(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 - 3x^4 + 4x^3 - 5x^2 + 2x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x^2 - 10x + x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5 \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 - x^4 + x^3 + x^2 - 6x - 5 \\]\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3)(x - 3) \\]\n     Use the difference of squares:\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)(x^2 - 9) \\]\n     First, compute \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)\\):\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4) = x^4 - 4x^2 - x^2 + 4 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 5x^2 + 4 \\]\n     Now, multiply by \\((x^2 - 9)\\):\n     \\[ (x^4 - 5x^2 + 4)(x^2 - 9) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^4(x^2 - 9) - 5x^2(x^2 - 9) + 4(x^2 - 9) \\]\n     \\[ = x^6 - 9x^4 - 5x^4 + 45x^2 + 4x^2 - 36 \\]\n     \\[ = x^6 - 14x^4 + 49x^2 - 36 \\]\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^7 - 2x^6 + 3x^5 - 4x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8) \\div (x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^7\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^5\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^5\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^7 - x^6 + x^5\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^7 - 2x^6 + 3x^5 - 4x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8) - (x^7 - x^6 + x^5) = -x^6 + 2x^5 - 4x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^6\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^4\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x^4\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^6 + x^5 - x^4\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^6 + 2x^5 - 4x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8) - (-x^6 + x^5 - x^4) = x^5 - 3x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8\\).\n     7. Divide \\(x^5\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^3\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(x^3\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^5 - x^4 + x^3\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((x^5 - 3x^4 + 5x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8) - (x^5 - x^4 + x^3) = -2x^4 + 4x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8\\).\n     10. Divide \\(-2x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2x^2\\).\n     11. Multiply \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^4 + 2x^3 - 2x^2\\).\n     12. Subtract: \\((-2x^4 + 4x^3 - 6x^2 + 7x - 8) - (-2x^4 + 2x^3 - 2x^2) = 2x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 8\\).\n     13. Divide \\(2x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(2x\\).\n     14. Multiply \\(2x\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(2x^3 - 2x^2 + 2x\\).\n     15. Subtract: \\((2x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 8) - (2x^3 - 2x^2 + 2x) = -2x^2 + 5x - 8\\).\n     16. Divide \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2\\).\n     17. Multiply \\(-2\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^2 + 2x - 2\\).\n     18. Subtract: \\((-2x^2 + 5x - 8) - (-2x^2 + 2x - 2) = 3x - 6\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - 2x^2 + 2x - 2\\) with a remainder of \\(3x - 6\\).\n\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^8 - 3x^7 + 2x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) \\div (x^2 - 2x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^8\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^6\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^6\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(x^8 - 2x^7 + x^6\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^8 - 3x^7 + 2x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (x^8 - 2x^7 + x^6) = -x^7 + x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^7\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^5\\).\n     5. Multiply \\(-x^5\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^7 + 2x^6 - x^5\\).\n     6. Subtract: \\((-x^7 + x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-x^7 + 2x^6 - x^5) = -x^6 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     7. Divide \\(-x^6\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-x^4\\).\n     8. Multiply \\(-x^4\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-x^6 + 2x^5 - x^4\\).\n     9. Subtract: \\((-x^6 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-x^6 + 2x^5 - x^4) = -2x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     10. Divide \\(-2x^5\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2x^3\\).\n     11. Multiply \\(-2x^3\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^5 + 4x^4 - 2x^3\\).\n     12. Subtract: \\((-2x^5 + 2x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-2x^5 + 4x^4 - 2x^3) = -2x^4 + x^3 + x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     13. Divide \\(-2x^4\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-2x^2\\).\n     14. Multiply \\(-2x^2\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-2x^4 + 4x^3 - 2x^2\\).\n     15. Subtract: \\((-2x^4 + x^3 + x^2 - x + 1) - (-2x^4 + 4x^3 - 2x^2) = -3x^3 + 3x^2 - x + 1\\).\n     16. Divide \\(-3x^3\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-3x\\).\n     17. Multiply \\(-3x\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-3x^3 + 6x^2 - 3x\\).\n     18. Subtract: \\((-3x^3 + 3x^2 - x + 1) - (-3x^3 + 6x^2 - 3x) = -3x^2 + 2x + 1\\).\n     19. Divide \\(-3x^2\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(-3\\).\n     20. Multiply \\(-3\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\) to get \\(-3x^2 + 6x - 3\\).\n     21. Subtract: \\((-3x^2 + 2x + 1) - (-3x^2 + 6x - 3) = -4x + 4\\).\n\n     The quotient is \\(x^6 - x^5 - 2x^4 + 2x^3 - 3x^2 - 3x - 3\\) with a remainder of \\(-4x + 4\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 13**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ 8 + 12 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     \\[ 8 + 12 = 20 \\]\n     \\[ 20 \\div 13 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 7 \\]\n     \\[ 20 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(8 + 12 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ 11 - 5 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     \\[ 11 - 5 = 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\div 13 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\]\n     \\[ 6 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(11 - 5 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 13**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 3 \\times 4 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     \\[ 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\div 13 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 12 \\]\n     \\[ 12 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(3 \\times 4 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ 5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     First, compute \\(5 \\times 6 \\pmod{13}\\):\n     \\[ 5 \\times 6 = 30 \\]\n     \\[ 30 \\div 13 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n     \\[ 30 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(5 \\times 6 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n     Now, compute \\(4 \\times 7 \\pmod{13}\\):\n     \\[ 4 \\times 7 = 28 \\]\n     \\[ 28 \\div 13 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n     \\[ 28 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(4 \\times 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n     Therefore, \\(5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 13**:\n   - Multiplicative Inverse of 3 Modulo 13:\n     We need \\(x\\) such that \\(3x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 3 \\times 9 = 27 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 3 modulo 13 is 9.\n\n   - Compute \\(7 \\times 8 \\pmod{13}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 8 modulo 13:\n     First, find the multiplicative inverse of 8 modulo 13. We need \\(y\\) such that \\(8y \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13}\\).\n     By testing values, we find:\n     \\[ 8 \\times 5 = 40 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, the multiplicative inverse of 8 modulo 13 is 5.\n\n     Now, compute \\(7 \\times 5 \\pmod{13}\\):\n     \\[ 7 \\times 5 = 35 \\]\n     \\[ 35 \\div 13 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 9 \\]\n     \\[ 35 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{13} \\]\n     So, \\(7 \\times 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{13}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n1. Addition: \\(7\\)\n2. Subtraction: \\(6\\)\n3. Multiplication: \\(12\\)\n4. Multiplication: \\(2\\)\n5. Multiplicative Inverse of 3: 9\n6. \\(7 \\times 8 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{13}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 8: 9\n\nFeel free to ask if you need further assistance or have any questions about these problems! <|User|>I'm really enjoying these problems. Let's keep challenging ourselves with more complex ones. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nAbsolutely! Keeping up with these complex problems will help you develop a deeper understanding of polynomials and modular arithmetic. Here are some even more advanced problems for you to tackle:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Add \\(x^6 + 2x^5 - 3x^4 + 4x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 7\\) and \\(3x^5 - 2x^4 + x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5\\).\n   - Subtract \\(2x^4 - x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5\\) from \\(x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply \\(x^2 + 2x + 1\\) by \\(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5\\).\n   - Multiply \\((x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3)(x - 3)(x + 4)(x - 4)\\).\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Divide \\(x^8 - 2x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\).\n   - Divide \\(x^9 - 3x^8 + 2x^7 - x^6 + x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1\\) by \\(x^2 - 2x + 1\\).\n\n### Modular Arithmetic Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction Modulo 17**:\n   - Compute \\(10 + 15 \\pmod{17}\\).\n   - Compute \\(13 - 7 \\pmod{17}\\).\n\n2. **Multiplication Modulo 17**:\n   - Compute \\(4 \\times 5 \\pmod{17}\\).\n   - Compute \\(6 \\times 7 \\times 8 \\pmod{17}\\).\n\n3. **Division Modulo 17**:\n   - Find the multiplicative inverse of 4 modulo 17.\n   - Compute \\(7 \\times 8 \\pmod{17}\\) using the multiplicative inverse of 8 modulo 17.\n\nLet's solve these step by step:\n\n### Polynomial Problems:\n1. **Addition and Subtraction**:\n   - Addition:\n     \\[ (x^6 + 2x^5 - 3x^4 + 4x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 7) + (3x^5 - 2x^4 + x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) \\]\n     Combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^6 + (2x^5 + 3x^5) + (-3x^4 - 2x^4) + (4x^3 + x^3) + (-5x^2 - 3x^2) + (6x + 4x) + (-7 - 5) \\]\n     \\[ = x^6 + 5x^5 - 5x^4 + 5x^3 - 8x^2 + 10x - 12 \\]\n\n   - Subtraction:\n     \\[ (x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9) - (2x^4 - x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + 5) \\]\n     Distribute the negative sign and combine like terms:\n     \\[ x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9 - 2x^4 + x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5 \\]\n     \\[ = x^7 + (3x^6) + (-4x^5) + (5x^4 - 2x^4) + (-6x^3 + x^3) + (7x^2 - 3x^2) + (-8x + 4x) + (9 - 5) \\]\n     \\[ = x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 3x^4 - 5x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + 4 \\]\n\n2. **Multiplication**:\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x^2 + 2x + 1)(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^2(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) + 2x(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) + 1(x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5) \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 - 3x^4 + 4x^3 - 5x^2 + 2x^4 - 6x^3 + 8x^2 - 10x + x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 5 \\]\n     \\[ = x^5 - x^4 + x^3 + x^2 - 6x - 5 \\]\n\n   - Multiplication:\n     \\[ (x + 1)(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 2)(x + 3)(x - 3)(x + 4)(x - 4) \\]\n     Use the difference of squares:\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)(x^2 - 9)(x^2 - 16)(x^2 - 25) \\]\n     First, compute \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4)\\):\n     \\[ (x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 4) = x^4 - 4x^2 - x^2 + 4 \\]\n     \\[ = x^4 - 5x^2 + 4 \\]\n     Now, multiply by \\((x^2 - 9)\\):\n     \\[ (x^4 - 5x^2 + 4)(x^2 - 9) \\]\n     Use the distributive property:\n     \\[ x^4(x^2 - 9) - 5x^2(x^2 - 9) + 4(x^2 - 9) \\]\n     \\[ = x^6 - 9x^4 - 5x^4 + 45x^2 + 4x^2 - 36 \\]\n     \\[ = x^6 - 14x^4 + 49x^2 - 36 \\]\n\n3. **Division**:\n   - Division:\n     \\[ (x^8 - 2x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9) \\div (x^2 - x + 1) \\]\n     Use polynomial long division:\n     1. Divide \\(x^8\\) by \\(x^2\\) to get \\(x^6\\).\n     2. Multiply \\(x^6\\) by \\(x^2 - x + 1\\) to get \\(x^8 - x^7 + x^6\\).\n     3. Subtract: \\((x^8 - 2x^7 + 3x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9) - (x^8 - x^7 + x^6) = -x^7 + 2x^6 - 4x^5 + 5x^4 - 6x^3 + 7x^2 - 8x + 9\\).\n     4. Divide \\(-x^7\\) by",
