de.\n\n```\n  40 | 5\n  -+---\n  20 | 3\n  -+---\n  0 | 0\n```\n\n2. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**\n   - The area of the first smaller rectangle (top left) is \\( 40 \\times 20 = 800 \\).\n   - The area of the second smaller rectangle (top right) is \\( 40 \\times 3 = 120 \\).\n   - The area of the third smaller rectangle (bottom left) is \\( 5 \\times 20 = 100 \\).\n   - The area of the fourth smaller rectangle (bottom right) is \\( 5 \\times 3 = 15 \\).\n\n3. **Add the areas of the smaller rectangles:**\n   - Add the results of the smaller rectangles:\n     \\[\n     800 + 120 + 100 + 15 = 1035\n     \\]\n\nSo, using the area model, we find that:\n\\[\n45 \\times 23 = 1035\n\\]\n\nThis method visually represents the multiplication and helps in understanding how each part of the multiplication contributes to the final result. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this using the lattice method for 45 and 23?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>.\nCertainly! Let's solve \\( 45 \\times 23 \\) using the lattice method.\n\n### Step-by-Step Lattice Method:\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid:**\n   - Draw a 2x2 grid because we are multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number.\n   - Label the top row with the digits of 45 (4 and 5).\n   - Label the left column with the digits of 23 (2 and 3).\n\n```\n  4 | 5\n  -+---\n  2 | 3\n  -+---\n  0 | 0\n```\n\n2. **Fill in the lattice grid:**\n   - Multiply each digit of the top row by each digit of the left column and write the result in the corresponding cell of the lattice.\n   - For the cell where the top row digit 4 meets the left column digit 2, write \\( 4 \\times 2 = 8 \\).\n   - For the cell where the top row digit 4 meets the left column digit 3, write \\( 4 \\times 3 = 12 \\).\n   - For the cell where the top row digit 5 meets the left column digit 2, write \\( 5 \\times 2 = 10 \\).\n   - For the cell where the top row digit 5 meets the left column digit 3, write \\( 5 \\times 3 = 15 \\).\n\n```\n  4 | 5\n  