tal weight of each type of fruit:.\n- Total Weight of Strawberries: \\(4 \\text{ cups} \\times 150 \\text{ grams/cup} = 600 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Blueberries: \\(3 \\text{ cups} \\times 100 \\text{ grams/cup} = 300 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Bananas: \\(2 \\text{ cups} \\times 150 \\text{ grams/cup} = 300 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Apples: \\(1 \\text{ cup} \\times 120 \\text{ grams/cup} = 120 \\text{ grams}\\).\n\nNext, calculate the total cost of each type of fruit:.\n- Total Cost of Strawberries: \\(4 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.50/\\text{cup} = \\$2.00\\).\n- Total Cost of Blueberries: \\(3 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.75/\\text{cup} = \\$2.25\\).\n- Total Cost of Bananas: \\(2 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.25/\\text{cup} = \\$0.50\\).\n- Total Cost of Apples: \\(1 \\text{ cup} \\times \\$0.30/\\text{cup} = \\$0.30\\).\n\nNow, calculate the total cost of all ingredients:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost} = \\$2.00 + \\$2.25 + \\$0.50 + \\$0.30 = \\$5.05 \\].\n\nNext, apply a 10% discount to the total cost:.\n\\[ \\text{Discount} = 0.10 \\times \\$5.05 = \\$0.505 \\].\n\nSubtract the discount from the total cost:.\n\\[ \\text{Final Cost} = \\$5.05 - \\$0.505 = \\$4.545 \\].\n\nFinally, calculate the total weight, total volume, total surface area, total energy content, total nutritional value, total cost per unit weight, and total cost per unit volume of all ingredients:.\n- Total Weight: \\(600 \\text{ grams} + 300 \\text{ grams} + 300 \\text{ grams} + 120 \\text{ grams} = 1320 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Volume: \\(100 \\text{ cubic centimeters} \\times 4 + 50 \\text{ cubic centimeters} \\times 3 + 120 \\text{ cubic centimeters} \\times 2 + 100 \\text{ cubic centimeters} \\times 1 = 400 + 150 + 240 + 100 = 990 \\text{ cubic centimeters}\\).\n- Total Surface Area: \\(200 \\text{ square centimeters} \\times 4 + 150 \\text{ square centimeters} \\times 3 + 180 \\text{ square centimeters} \\times 2 + 160 \\text{ square centimeters} \\times 1 = 800 + 450 + 360 + 160 = 1770 \\text{ square centimeters}\\).\n- Total Energy Content: \\(100 \\text{ calories/cup} \\times 4 + 80 \\text{ calories/cup} \\times 3 + 120 \\text{ calories/cup} \\times 2 + 90 \\text{ calories/cup} \\times 1 = 400 + 240 + 240 + 90 = 1070 \\text{ calories}\\).\n- Total Nutritional Value: \\(50 \\text{ units/cup} \\times 4 + 40 \\text{ units/cup} \\times 3 + 30 \\text{ units/cup} \\times 2 + 20 \\text{ units/cup} \\times 1 = 200 + 120 + 60 + 20 = 380 \\text{ units}\\).\n\nCalculate the total cost per unit weight:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost per Unit Weight} = \\frac{\\text{Total Cost}}{\\text{Total Weight}} = \\frac{\\$5.05}{1320 \\text{ grams}} \\approx \\$0.00383 \\text{ per gram} \\].\n\nCalculate the total cost per unit volume:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost per Unit Volume} = \\frac{\\text{Total Cost}}{\\text{Total Volume}} = \\frac{\\$5.05}{990 \\text{ cubic centimeters}} \\approx \\$0.00511 \\text{ per cubic centimeter} \\].\n\nSo, the final cost of the ingredients for the fruit salad after applying the discount is $4.545, the total weight of the ingredients is 1320 grams, the total volume of the ingredients is 990 cubic centimeters, the total surface area of the ingredients is 1770 square centimeters, the total energy content of the ingredients is 1070 calories, the total nutritional value of the ingredients is 380 units, the total cost per unit weight of the ingredients is approximately $0.00383 per gram, and the total cost per unit volume of the ingredients is approximately $0.00511 per cubic centimeter. This method simplifies the calculation by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable multiplications, additions, subtractions, divisions, and multiplications, making it easier to compute the final cost, total weight, total volume, total surface area, total energy content, total nutritional value, total cost per unit weight, and total cost per unit volume after applying the discount. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distribu