s, an energy content of 80 calories per cup, and a nutritional value of 40 units per cup.\n- 2 cups of bananas, each weighing 150 grams and having a volume of 120 cubic centimeters, a surface area of 180 square centimeters, an energy content of 120 calories per cup, and a nutritional value of 30 units per cup.\n- 1 cup of apples, each weighing 120 grams and having a volume of 100 cubic centimeters, a surface area of 160 square centimeters, an energy content of 90 calories per cup, and a nutritional value of 20 units per cup.\n\nFirst, calculate the total weight of each type of fruit:.\n- Total Weight of Strawberries: \\(4 \\text{ cups} \\times 150 \\text{ grams/cup} = 600 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Blueberries: \\(3 \\text{ cups} \\times 100 \\text{ grams/cup} = 300 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Bananas: \\(2 \\text{ cups} \\times 150 \\text{ grams/cup} = 300 \\text{ grams}\\).\n- Total Weight of Apples: \\(1 \\text{ cup} \\times 120 \\text{ grams/cup} = 120 \\text{ grams}\\).\n\nNext, calculate the total cost of each type of fruit:.\n- Total Cost of Strawberries: \\(4 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.50/\\text{cup} = \\$2.00\\).\n- Total Cost of Blueberries: \\(3 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.75/\\text{cup} = \\$2.25\\).\n- Total Cost of Bananas: \\(2 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$0.25/\\text{cup} = \\$0.50\\).\n- Total Cost of Apples: \\(1 \\text{ cup} \\times \\$0.30/\\text{cup} = \\$0.30\\).\n\nNow, calculate the total cost of all ingredients:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost} = \\$2.00 + \\$2.25 + \\$0.50 + \\$0.30 = \\$5.05 \\].\n\nNext, apply a 10% discount to the total cost:.\n\\[ \\text{Discount} = 0.10 \\times \\$5.05 = \\$0.505 \\].\n\nSubtract the discount from the total cost:.\n\\[ \\text{Final Cost} = \\$5.05 - \\$0.505 = \\$4.545 \\].\n\nFinally, calculate the total weight, total volume, total surface area, total energy content, total nutritional value, total cost per unit weight, total cost per unit volume, and total cost per unit area of all ingredients