rtainly! Breaking down numbers into factors that are easier to work with is particularly useful in situations where the numbers are large or complex, making direct multiplication difficult or prone to errors. Here's an example where this strategy would be particularly helpful:\n\n### Example:\nSuppose you need to calculate the product of 123 and 456.\n\n1. **Direct Multiplication:**\n   - Directly multiplying 123 by 456 would involve a lot of steps and could be error-prone.\n   - \\( 123 \\times 456 = 56088 \\)\n\n2. **Breaking Down the Numbers:**\n   - Break down 123 into 100 + 20 + 3.\n   - Break down 456 into 400 + 50 + 6.\n   - Use the distributive property to break the multiplication into smaller parts:\n     \\[\n     123 \\times 456 = (100 + 20 + 3) \\times (400 + 50 + 6)\n     \\]\n   - Apply the distributive property:\n     \\[\n     = 100 \\times 400 + 100 \\times 50 + 100 \\times 6 + 20 \\times 400 + 20 \\times 50 + 20 \\times 6 + 3 \\times 400 + 3 \\times 50 + 3 \\times 6\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     = 40000 + 5000 + 600 + 8000 + 1000 + 120 + 1200 + 150 + 18\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     = 40000 + 5000 + 600 + 8000 + 1000 + 120 + 1200 + 150 + 18 = 56088\n     \\]\n\nBy breaking down the numbers into 100 + 20 + 3 and 400 + 50 + 6, we can perform the multiplication in smaller, more manageable parts, reducing the complexity and the risk of errors.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThis strategy is particularly useful when dealing with large numbers or complex multiplications, as it breaks the problem into simpler components, making the calculation more straightforward and less prone to errors. This method is especially helpful in situations where direct multiplication is cumbersome or prone to mistakes. </think>.\n<answer>.\nBreaking down numbers into factors that are easier to work with is particularly useful in situations where the numbers are large or complex, making direct multiplication difficult or prone to errors. An example where this strategy would be particularly helpful is when calculating the product of 123 and 456.\n\n1. **Direct Multiplication:**\n   - Directly multiplying 123 by 456 would involve a lot of steps and could be error-prone.\n   - \\( 123 \\times 456 = 56088 \\)\n\n2. **Breaking Down the Numbers:**\n   - Break down 123 into 100 + 20 + 3.\n   - Break down 456 into 400 + 50 + 6.\n   - Use the distributive property to break the multiplication into smaller parts:\n     \\[\n     123 \\times 456 = (100 + 20 + 3) \\times (400 + 50 + 6)\n     \\]\n   - Apply the distributive property:\n     \\[\n     = 100 \\times 400 + 100 \\times 50 + 100 \\times 6 + 20 \\times 400 + 20 \\times 50 + 20 \\times 6 + 3 \\times 400 + 3 \\times 50 + 3 \\times 6\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     = 40000 + 5000 + 600 + 8000 + 1000 + 120 + 1200 + 150 + 18\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     = 40000 + 5000 + 600 + 8000 + 1000 + 120 + 1200 + 150 + 18 = 56088\n     \\]\n\nBy breaking down the numbers into 100 + 20 + 3 and 400 + 50 + 6, we can perform the multiplication in smaller, more manageable parts, reducing the complexity and the risk of errors. This method is especially helpful in situations where direct multiplication is cumbersome or prone to mistakes.\n</answer>",
