      "index": 0,
      "text": "148 + 1480 = 1628.\n```.\nSo, the product of 37 and 44 is 1628.\n</answer>.<|User|>That's correct! Can you also show me how to solve this using the distributive property of multiplication over addition? I find it helpful to understand different methods.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>\nCertainly! Using the distributive property of multiplication over addition can simplify the multiplication process. The distributive property states that \\(a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c\\). We can apply this property to break down the multiplication of 37 and 44 into more manageable parts. Specifically, we can express 44 as \\(40 + 4\\), and then distribute the multiplication over these two parts. Let's go through the steps.\n..\n</think>\n<answer>\nCertainly! Using the distributive property, we can express the multiplication of 37 and 44 as follows:\n\\[ 37 \\times 44 = 37 \\times (40 + 4) \\]\nBy the distributive property, we can distribute the multiplication over the addition:\n\\[ 37 \\times (40 + 4) = 37 \\times 40 + 37 \\times 4 \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate each part separately:\n1. Calculate \\(37 \\times 40\\):\n\\[ 37 \\times 40 = 37 \\times (4 \\times 10) = (37 \\times 4) \\times 10 \\]\nFirst, find \\(37 \\times 4\\):\n\\[ 37 \\times 4 = 148 \\]\nThen, multiply by 10:\n\\[ 148 \\times 10 = 1480 \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(37 \\times 4\\):\n\\[ 37 \\times 4 = 148 \\]\n\nNow, add the two results together:\n\\[ 1480 + 148 = 1628 \\]\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we have:\n\\[ 37 \\times 44 = 1480 + 148 = 1628 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 37 and 44 is \\(\\boxed{1628}\\).\n</answer>",
