   - - - -\n   9 | 4\n   ```\n\n2. **Divide each cell in the grid:**\n   - Each cell is divided into two triangles, one for the tens place and one for the ones place.\n\n3. **Multiply the digits:**\n   - Multiply the digits in each cell and write the result in the appropriate triangle.\n   - For the top-left cell (7 \u00d7 9):\n     ```\n     7 | 0\n     - - - -\n     9 | 4\n     ```\n     7 \u00d7 9 = 63. Write 6 in the tens triangle and 3 in the ones triangle.\n   - For the top-right cell (7 \u00d7 4):\n     ```\n     7 | 0\n     - - - -\n     9 | 4\n     ```\n     7 \u00d7 4 = 28. Write 2 in the tens triangle and 8 in the ones triangle.\n   - For the bottom-left cell (0 \u00d7 9):\n     ```\n     7 | 0\n     - - - -\n     9 | 4\n     ```\n     0 \u00d7 9 = 0. Write 0 in the tens triangle and 0 in the ones triangle.\n   - For the bottom-right cell (0 \u00d7 4):\n     ```\n     7 | 0\n     - - - -\n     9 | 4\n     ```\n     0 \u00d7 4 = 0. Write 0 in the tens triangle and 0 in the ones triangle.\n\n   The completed lattice looks like this:\n   ```\n   6 | 3\n   - - - -\n   2 | 8\n   0 | 0\n   0 | 0\n   ```\n\n4. **Sum the diagonals:**\n   - Start from the right and move to the left, summing the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - The rightmost diagonal (0 + 0 + 0) is 0.\n   - The next diagonal (3 + 8 + 0) is 11. Write 1 and carry over 1 to the next diagonal.\n   - The next diagonal (6 + 2 + 1) is 9. Add the carried over 1 to get 10. Write 0 and carry over 1.\n   - The leftmost diagonal (6 + 0) is 6. Add the carried over 1 to get 7.\n\n   The final sum is:\n   ```\n   6 | 3\n   - - - -\n   2 | 8\n   0 | 0\n   0 | 0\n   7 | 0\n   ```\n\n5. **Read the result:**\n   - The final product is read from the bottom-left to the top-right.\n   - The result is 6580.\n\nSo, using the lattice method, we also arrive at the same result: 6580.\n\nThis method provides a structured way to handle multiplication, making it easier to manage and verify the calculations, especially for larger