0) + (28 \\times 1)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate each part separately**:\n   - First, calculate \\(28 \\times 50\\):\n     \\[\n     28 \\times 50 = 1400\n     \\]\n   - Next, calculate \\(28 \\times 1\\):\n     \\[\n     28 \\times 1 = 28\n     \\]\n\n4. **Add the results together**:\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 51 = 1400 + 28 = 1428\n   \\]\n\nSo, the product of 28 and 51 is 1428.\n\nTo summarize, the process is the same as before, but we need to adjust the second part of the sum to include the additional 1. This results in:\n\\[\n28 \\times 51 = (28 \\times 50) + (28 \\times 1) = 1400 + 28 = 1428\n\\]\n\nThis shows that the distributive property allows us to handle the multiplication of a number with a sum by breaking it into simpler multiplications and additions. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1428}\n\\]",
