      "text": "2532. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanations and examples. Can you provide another example where the lattice method might be more advantageous? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider a slightly larger example to illustrate when the lattice method might be more advantageous. Suppose we want to multiply 123 by 456 using the lattice method.\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid:**.\n   - Draw a 3x3 grid because we are multiplying a 3-digit number by a 3-digit number.\n\n```.\n  1 | 2 | 3.\n  - + - + -.\n  4 | 5 | 6.\n```.\n\n2. **Divide each cell into two triangles:**.\n   - Each cell will have a diagonal line dividing it into two parts.\n\n```.\n  1 | 2 | 3.\n  - + - + -.\n  4 | 5 | 6.\n```.\n\n3. **Multiply the digits:**.\n   - Multiply the digits in each cell and write the result in the appropriate triangle. If the product is a single digit, place it in the top triangle; if it's a two-digit number, place the tens digit in the top triangle and the ones digit in the bottom triangle.\n\n```.\n  1 | 2 | 3.\n  - + - + -.\n  4 | 5 | 6.\n  04 | 10 | 18.\n  08 | 12 | 18.\n```.\n\n4. **Add the diagonals:**.\n   - Starting from the bottom right, add the numbers in the diagonals. If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n\n```.\n  1 | 2 | 3.\n  - + - + -.\n  4 | 5 | 6.\n  04 | 10 | 18.\n  08 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 | 18.\n  01 | 12 |