w to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(67 \\times 54\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(67\\) can be broken down into \\(60 + 7\\).\n   - \\(54\\) can be broken down into \\(50 + 4\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(60 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(60 \\times 4\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 4\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(60 \\times 50 = 3000\\).\n   - \\(60 \\times 4 = 240\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 50 = 350\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 4 = 28\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     3000 + 240 + 350 + 28 = 3618.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 67 and 54 is 3618. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(67 \\times 54\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 67 along the top of the grid and the digits of 54 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 6 by 5, 6 by 4, 7 by 5, and 7 by 4.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   6 | 28.\n   7 | 35.\n   7 | 28.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   6 | 28.\n   7 | 35.\n   7 | 28.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ──