 each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(70 \\times 40\\).\n   - \\(70 \\times 3\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 40\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 3\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(70 \\times 40 = 2800\\).\n   - \\(70 \\times 3 = 210\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 40 = 320\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 3 = 24\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     2800 + 210 + 320 + 24 = 3354.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 78 and 43 is 3354. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(78 \\times 43\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 78 along the top of the grid and the digits of 43 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   7 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 3.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 7 by 4, 7 by 3, 8 by 4, and 8 by 3.\n   ```.\n   7 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 3.\n   7 | 24.\n   8 | 32.\n   8 | 24.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   7 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 3.\n   7 | 24.\n   8 | 32.\n   8 | 24.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```.\n   7 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 3.\n   7 | 24.\n   8 | 32.\n   8 | 24.\n   ───┼───.\n   280 | 240.\n   ```.\n\n5. **Read the final product from the top left to the bottom right**:.\n   - The final product is 3354.\n\nSo, using the grid method, the product of 78 and 43 is 3354. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 92 and 65? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(92 \\times 65\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:.\n   ```.\n   92.\n× 65.\n```.\n\n2. **Multiply 92 by the units digit of 65 (which is 5)**:.\n   - First, multiply 92 by 5:.\n     ```.\n     92.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     460.\n     ```.\n   - Write 460 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n3. **Multiply 92 by the tens digit of 65 (which is 6), and remember to shift one place to the left**:.\n   - First, multiply 92 by 6:.\n     ```.\n     92.\n   ×  6.\n   -----.\n     552.\n     ```.\n   - Since we are multiplying by the tens place, we shift the result one place to the left:.\n     ```.\n     92.\n   ×  6.\n   -----.\n     5520.\n     ```.\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:.\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:.\n     ```.\n     92.\n   ×  65.\n   -----.\n     460.\n   +5520.\n   -----.\n     6440.\n     ```.\n\n5. **The final product is**:.\n   ```.\n   92.\n   × 65.\n   -----.\n   6440.\n   ```.\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 92 and 65 is 6440. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(92 \\times 65\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(92\\) can be broken down into \\(90 + 2\\).\n   - \\(65\\) can be broken down into \\(60 + 5\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(90 \\times 60\\).\n   - \\(90 \\times 5\\).\n   - \\(2 \\times 60\\).\n   - \\(2 \\times 5\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(90 \\times 60 = 5400\\).\n   - \\(90 \\times 5 = 450\\).\n   - \\(2 \\times 60 = 120\\).\n   - \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     5400 + 450 + 120 + 10 = 5980.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 92 and 65 is 5980. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(92 \\times 65\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 92 along the top of the grid and the digits of 65 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   9 | 2.\n   ───┼───.\n   6 | 5.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 9 by 6, 9 by 5, 2 by 6, and 2 by 5.\n   ```.\n   9 | 2.\n   ───┼───.\n   6 | 5.\n   9 | 10.\n   2 | 12.\n   2 | 10.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   9 | 2.\n   ───┼───.\n   6 | 5.\n   9 | 10.\n   2 | 12.\n   2 | 10.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```.\n   9 | 2.\n   ───┼───.\n   6 | 5.\n   9 | 10.\n   2 | 12.\n   2 | 10.\n   ───┼───.\n   540 | 120.\n   ```.\n\n5. **Read the final product from the top left to the bottom right**:.\n   - The final product is 5980.\n\nSo, using the grid method, the product of 92 and 65 is 5980. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 81 and 76? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(81 \\times 76\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:.\n   ```.\n   81.\n× 76.\n```.\n\n2. **Multiply 81 by the units digit of 76 (which is 6)**:.\n   - First, multiply 81 by 6:.\n     ```.\n     81.\