      "text": "812.</answer>.<|User|>The answer is correct. Here's another way to solve it: 14 \u00d7 58 = 14 \u00d7 (60 - 2) = 14 \u00d7 60 - 14 \u00d7 2 = 840 - 28 = 812. This method is called the distributive property of multiplication over subtraction. Please explain the distributive property in more detail.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>\nThe distributive property of multiplication over addition or subtraction is a fundamental concept in arithmetic and algebra. It states that multiplying a number by a sum or difference is the same as multiplying the number by each term in the sum or difference and then adding or subtracting those products. Mathematically, it can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c \\]\n\\[ a \\times (b - c) = a \\times b - a \\times c \\]\n\nIn the given problem, \\( 14 \\times 58 \\), we can use the distributive property to break down the multiplication into simpler steps. The number 58 can be expressed as \\( 60 - 2 \\). This allows us to rewrite the original problem as:\n\n\\[ 14 \\times 58 = 14 \\times (60 - 2) \\]\n\nUsing the distributive property, we can distribute the 14 across the terms inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[ 14 \\times (60 - 2) = 14 \\times 60 - 14 \\times 2 \\]\n\nNow, we can calculate each term separately:\n\n\\[ 14 \\times 60 = 840 \\]\n\\[ 14 \\times 2 = 28 \\]\n\nSubtracting these results gives us:\n\n\\[ 840 - 28 = 812 \\]\n\nThis confirms that the product of 14 and 58 is indeed 812.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe distributive property of multiplication over subtraction is a powerful tool that simplifies the process of multiplication by breaking down the problem into smaller, more manageable parts. In the given problem, we used the distributive property to rewrite \\( 14 \\times 58 \\) as \\( 14 \\times (60 - 2) \\), which is equivalent to \\( 14 \\times 60 - 14 \\times 2 \\). By calculating each term separately and then subtracting, we arrived at the same result, 812, as we did using the standard multiplication method. This method not only helps in understanding the underlying structure of the multiplication but also provides an alternative way to solve the problem.\n</answer>",
