      "text": "735.</answer>.<|User|>The answer is correct. Thank you for walking me through the process step by step. It really helped me understand how to solve this multiplication problem.<|Assistant|>You're welcome! I'm glad to have helped you understand the process of multiplying 49 by 15. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any mathematical concept, feel free to ask. Understanding the steps and reasoning behind calculations is crucial for building a strong foundation in mathematics. Good luck with your studies! <|User|>Thank you. I have another question. How do you solve a system of linear equations using the substitution method? Could you walk me through an example? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! The substitution method is a great way to solve systems of linear equations. It involves solving one of the equations for one variable and then substituting that expression into the other equation to solve for the other variable. After finding one variable, you can substitute it back into one of the original equations to find the other variable. Let's go through an example to illustrate this process.\nConsider the following system of linear equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 5 \\)\n2. \\( 2x - y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ x + y = 5 \\]\nSubtract \\( x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ y = 5 - x \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( y \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2x - y = 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = 5 - x \\):\n\\[ 2x - (5 - x) = 1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x - 5 + x = 1 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 3x - 5 = 1 \\]\nAdd 5 to both sides:\n\\[ 3x = 6 \\]\nDivide by 3:\n\\[ x = 2 \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( x \\) back into one of the original equations to find \\( y \\). Let's use the first equation:\n\\[ x + y = 5 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 2 \\):\n\\[ 2 + y = 5 \\]\nSubtract 2 from both sides:\n\\[ y = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( y = 3 \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 5 \\)\n\\[ 2 + 3 = 5 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x - y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 2(2) - 3 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 4 - 3 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( (2, 3) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( x + y = 5 \\) and \\( 2x - y = 1 \\) is \\( (2, 3) \\).</answer> <|User|>That was very helpful. Can you provide another example with different numbers to ensure I understand the process clearly? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider another example with different numbers. Suppose we have the following system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x - y = 1 \\]\nAdd \\( y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ x = y + 1 \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 3x + 2y = 12 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = y + 1 \\):\n\\[ 3(y + 1) + 2y = 12 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 3y + 3 + 2y = 12 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 5y + 3 = 12 \\]\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\\[ 5y = 9 \\]\nDivide by 5:\n\\[ y = \\frac{9}{5} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = y + 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{9}{5} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{9}{5} + 1 \\]\nConvert 1 to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[ x = \\frac{9}{5} + \\frac{5}{5} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{14}{5} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{14}{5} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{9}{5} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 12 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) + 2\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) = 12 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{42}{5} + \\frac{18}{5} = 12 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{60}{5} = 12 \\]\n\\[ 12 = 12 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( x - y = 1 \\)\n\\[ \\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{9}{5} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{5}{5} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 3x + 2y = 12 \\) and \\( x - y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation and the new example. It really helps to see the process in action. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the substitution method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nOf course! Let's consider a different system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 2x + y = 7 \\)\n2. \\( x - 3y = -1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 2x + y = 7 \\]\nSubtract \\( 2x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ y = 7 - 2x \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( y \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ x - 3y = -1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = 7 - 2x \\):\n\\[ x - 3(7 - 2x) = -1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x - 21 + 6x = -1 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 7x - 21 = -1 \\]\nAdd 21 to both sides:\n\\[ 7x = 20 \\]\nDivide by 7:\n\\[ x = \\frac{20}{7} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( x \\) back into the expression we found for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 7 - 2x \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{20}{7} \\):\n\\[ y = 7 - 2\\left(\\frac{20}{7}\\right) \\]\nConvert 2 to a fraction with a denominator of 7:\n\\[ y = 7 - \\frac{40}{7} \\]\nConvert 7 to a fraction with a denominator of 7:\n\\[ y = \\frac{49}{7} - \\frac{40}{7} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ y = \\frac{9}{7} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{20}{7} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{9}{7} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 2x + y = 7 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{20}{7}\\right) + \\frac{9}{7} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{40}{7} + \\frac{9}{7} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{49}{7} = 7 \\]\n\\[ 7 = 7 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( x - 3y = -1 \\)\n\\[ \\frac{20}{7} - 3\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right) = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{20}{7} - \\frac{27}{7} = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{-7}{7} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -1 = -1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{20}{7}, \\frac{9}{7}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 2x + y = 7 \\) and \\( x - 3y = -1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{20}{7}, \\frac{9}{7}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 4x - 2y = 10 \\)\n2. \\( 2x + 3y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 4x - 2y = 10 \\]\nSubtract \\( 4x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ -2y = 10 - 4x \\]\nDivide by -2:\n\\[ y = -5 + 2x \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( y \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2x + 3y = 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = -5 + 2x \\):\n\\[ 2x + 3(-5 + 2x) = 1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x - 15 + 6x = 1 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 8x - 15 = 1 \\]\nAdd 15 to both sides:\n\\[ 8x = 16 \\]\nDivide by 8:\n\\[ x = 2 \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( x \\) back into the expression we found for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = -5 + 2x \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 2 \\):\n\\[ y = -5 + 2(2) \\]\n\\[ y = -5 + 4 \\]\n\\[ y = -1 \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( y = -1 \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 4x - 2y = 10 \\)\n\\[ 4(2) - 2(-1) = 10 \\]\n\\[ 8 + 2 = 10 \\]\n\\[ 10 = 10 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x + 3y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 2(2) + 3(-1) = 1 \\]\n\\[ 4 - 3 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( (2, -1) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 4x - 2y = 10 \\) and \\( 2x + 3y = 1 \\) is \\( (2, -1) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nOf course! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 4y = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x - 2y = 1 \\]\nAdd \\( 2y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ x = 1 + 2y \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 3x + 4y = 12 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 1 + 2y \\):\n\\[ 3(1 + 2y) + 4y = 12 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 3 + 6y + 4y = 12 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 3 + 10y = 12 \\]\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\\[ 10y = 9 \\]\nDivide by 10:\n\\[ y = \\frac{9}{10} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 1 + 2y \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{9}{10} \\):\n\\[ x = 1 + 2\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\]\nConvert 1 to a fraction with a denominator of 10:\n\\[ x = \\frac{10}{10} + \\frac{18}{10} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{28}{10} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{14}{5} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{14}{5} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{9}{10} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 4y = 12 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) + 4\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right) = 12 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{42}{5} + \\frac{36}{10} = 12 \\]\nConvert \\(\\frac{36}{10}\\) to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[ \\frac{42}{5} + \\frac{18}{5} = 12 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{60}{5} = 12 \\]\n\\[ 12 = 12 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\\[ \\frac{14}{5} - 2\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right) = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{18}{10} = 1 \\]\nConvert \\(\\frac{18}{10}\\) to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[ \\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{9}{5} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{5}{5} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 3x + 4y = 12 \\) and \\( x - 2y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 3y = 7 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 2 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\nAdd \\( y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ x = y + 2 \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 2x + 3y = 7 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = y + 2 \\):\n\\[ 2(y + 2) + 3y = 7 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 2y + 4 + 3y = 7 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 5y + 4 = 7 \\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[ 5y = 3 \\]\nDivide by 5:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = y + 2 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{3}{5} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{5} + 2 \\]\nConvert 2 to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{10}{5} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{13}{5} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{13}{5} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{3}{5} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 3y = 7 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{13}{5}\\right) + 3\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{26}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{35}{5} = 7 \\]\n\\[ 7 = 7 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( x - y = 2 \\)\n\\[ \\frac{13}{5} - \\frac{3}{5} = 2 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{10}{5} = 2 \\]\n\\[ 2 = 2 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{13}{5}, \\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 2x + 3y = 7 \\) and \\( x - y = 2 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{13}{5}, \\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 5x - 2y = 11 \\)\n2. \\( 3x + y = 7 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 3x + y = 7 \\]\nSubtract \\( 3x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ y = 7 - 3x \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( y \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 5x - 2y = 11 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = 7 - 3x \\):\n\\[ 5x - 2(7 - 3x) = 11 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 5x - 14 + 6x = 11 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 11x - 14 = 11 \\]\nAdd 14 to both sides:\n\\[ 11x = 25 \\]\nDivide by 11:\n\\[ x = \\frac{25}{11} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( x \\) back into the expression we found for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 7 - 3x \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{25}{11} \\):\n\\[ y = 7 - 3\\left(\\frac{25}{11}\\right) \\]\nConvert 3 to a fraction with a denominator of 11:\n\\[ y = 7 - \\frac{75}{11} \\]\nConvert 7 to a fraction with a denominator of 11:\n\\[ y = \\frac{77}{11} - \\frac{75}{11} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ y = \\frac{2}{11} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{25}{11} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{2}{11} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 5x - 2y = 11 \\)\n\\[ 5\\left(\\frac{25}{11}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{2}{11}\\right) = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{125}{11} - \\frac{4}{11} = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{121}{11} = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 3x + y = 7 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{25}{11}\\right) + \\frac{2}{11} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{75}{11} + \\frac{2}{11} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{77}{11} = 7 \\]\n\\[ 7 = 7 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{25}{11}, \\frac{2}{11}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 5x - 2y = 11 \\) and \\( 3x + y = 7 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{25}{11}, \\frac{2}{11}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 4x + 3y = 16 \\)\n2. \\( 2x - y = 4 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 2x - y = 4 \\]\nSubtract \\( 2x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ -y = 4 - 2x \\]\nMultiply by -1:\n\\[ y = 2x - 4 \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( y \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 4x + 3y = 16 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = 2x - 4 \\):\n\\[ 4x + 3(2x - 4) = 16 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 4x + 6x - 12 = 16 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 10x - 12 = 16 \\]\nAdd 12 to both sides:\n\\[ 10x = 28 \\]\nDivide by 10:\n\\[ x = \\frac{28}{10} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{14}{5} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( x \\) back into the expression we found for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 2x - 4 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{14}{5} \\):\n\\[ y = 2\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) - 4 \\]\nConvert 4 to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[ y = \\frac{28}{5} - \\frac{20}{5} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ y = \\frac{8}{5} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{14}{5} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{8}{5} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 4x + 3y = 16 \\)\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) + 3\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right) = 16 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{56}{5} + \\frac{24}{5} = 16 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{80}{5} = 16 \\]\n\\[ 16 = 16 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x - y = 4 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) - \\frac{8}{5} = 4 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{28}{5} - \\frac{8}{5} = 4 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{20}{5} = 4 \\]\n\\[ 4 = 4 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{8}{5}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 4x + 3y = 16 \\) and \\( 2x - y = 4 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{14}{5}, \\frac{8}{5}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\)\n2. \\( x - 4y = -1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x - 4y = -1 \\]\nAdd \\( 4y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ x = 4y - 1 \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 3x + 2y = 11 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 4y - 1 \\):\n\\[ 3(4y - 1) + 2y = 11 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 12y - 3 + 2y = 11 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 14y - 3 = 11 \\]\nAdd 3 to both sides:\n\\[ 14y = 14 \\]\nDivide by 14:\n\\[ y = 1 \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 4y - 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = 1 \\):\n\\[ x = 4(1) - 1 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 - 1 \\]\n\\[ x = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\)\n\\[ 3(3) + 2(1) = 11 \\]\n\\[ 9 + 2 = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( x - 4y = -1 \\)\n\\[ 3 - 4(1) = -1 \\]\n\\[ 3 - 4 = -1 \\]\n\\[ -1 = -1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( (3, 1) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\) and \\( x - 4y = -1 \\) is \\( (3, 1) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 5y = 14 \\)\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x + 5y = 14 \\]\nSubtract \\( 5y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 2x = 14 - 5y \\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[ x = 7 - \\frac{5y}{2} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 3x - 2y = 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 7 - \\frac{5y}{2} \\):\n\\[ 3\\left(7 - \\frac{5y}{2}\\right) - 2y = 1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 21 - \\frac{15y}{2} - 2y = 1 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 21 - \\frac{15y}{2} - \\frac{4y}{2} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 21 - \\frac{19y}{2} = 1 \\]\nSubtract 21 from both sides:\n\\[ -\\frac{19y}{2} = 1 - 21 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{19y}{2} = -20 \\]\nMultiply by -2:\n\\[ 19y = 40 \\]\nDivide by 19:\n\\[ y = \\frac{40}{19} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 7 - \\frac{5y}{2} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{40}{19} \\):\n\\[ x = 7 - \\frac{5\\left(\\frac{40}{19}\\right)}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = 7 - \\frac{200}{38} \\]\n\\[ x = 7 - \\frac{100}{19} \\]\nConvert 7 to a fraction with a denominator of 19:\n\\[ x = \\frac{133}{19} - \\frac{100}{19} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{33}{19} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{33}{19} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{40}{19} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 5y = 14 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{33}{19}\\right) + 5\\left(\\frac{40}{19}\\right) = 14 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{66}{19} + \\frac{200}{19} = 14 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{266}{19} = 14 \\]\n\\[ 14 = 14 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{33}{19}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{40}{19}\\right) = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{99}{19} - \\frac{80}{19} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{19}{19} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{33}{19}, \\frac{40}{19}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 2x + 5y = 14 \\) and \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{33}{19}, \\frac{40}{19}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 4x - 3y = 7 \\)\n2. \\( 2x + 5y = 11 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x + 5y = 11 \\]\nSubtract \\( 5y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 2x = 11 - 5y \\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 5y}{2} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 4x - 3y = 7 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{11 - 5y}{2} \\):\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{11 - 5y}{2}\\right) - 3y = 7 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 2(11 - 5y) - 3y = 7 \\]\n\\[ 22 - 10y - 3y = 7 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 22 - 13y = 7 \\]\nSubtract 22 from both sides:\n\\[ -13y = 7 - 22 \\]\n\\[ -13y = -15 \\]\nDivide by -13:\n\\[ y = \\frac{15}{13} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 5y}{2} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{15}{13} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 5\\left(\\frac{15}{13}\\right)}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - \\frac{75}{13}}{2} \\]\nConvert 11 to a fraction with a denominator of 13:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{143}{13} - \\frac{75}{13}}{2} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{68}{13}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{68}{13} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{68}{26} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{34}{13} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{34}{13} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{15}{13} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 4x - 3y = 7 \\)\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{34}{13}\\right) - 3\\left(\\frac{15}{13}\\right) = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{136}{13} - \\frac{45}{13} = 7 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{91}{13} = 7 \\]\n\\[ 7 = 7 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x + 5y = 11 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{34}{13}\\right) + 5\\left(\\frac{15}{13}\\right) = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{68}{13} + \\frac{75}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{143}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{34}{13}, \\frac{15}{13}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 4x - 3y = 7 \\) and \\( 2x + 5y = 11 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{34}{13}, \\frac{15}{13}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\)\n2. \\( 2x - 3y = -1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 3x + 2y = 11 \\]\nSubtract \\( 2y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 3x = 11 - 2y \\]\nDivide by 3:\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 2y}{3} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2x - 3y = -1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{11 - 2y}{3} \\):\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{11 - 2y}{3}\\right) - 3y = -1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ \\frac{2(11 - 2y)}{3} - 3y = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{22 - 4y}{3} - 3y = -1 \\]\nMultiply through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 22 - 4y - 9y = -3 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 22 - 13y = -3 \\]\nSubtract 22 from both sides:\n\\[ -13y = -25 \\]\nDivide by -13:\n\\[ y = \\frac{25}{13} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 2y}{3} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{25}{13} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 2\\left(\\frac{25}{13}\\right)}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - \\frac{50}{13}}{3} \\]\nConvert 11 to a fraction with a denominator of 13:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{143}{13} - \\frac{50}{13}}{3} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{93}{13}}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{93}{13} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{93}{39} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{31}{13} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{31}{13} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{25}{13} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{31}{13}\\right) + 2\\left(\\frac{25}{13}\\right) = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{93}{13} + \\frac{50}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{143}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x - 3y = -1 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{31}{13}\\right) - 3\\left(\\frac{25}{13}\\right) = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{62}{13} - \\frac{75}{13} = -1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{-13}{13} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -1 = -1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{31}{13}, \\frac{25}{13}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 3x + 2y = 11 \\) and \\( 2x - 3y = -1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{31}{13}, \\frac{25}{13}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 5x - 3y = 14 \\)\n2. \\( 2x + 4y = 10 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x + 4y = 10 \\]\nSubtract \\( 4y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 2x = 10 - 4y \\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[ x = 5 - 2y \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 5x - 3y = 14 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = 5 - 2y \\):\n\\[ 5(5 - 2y) - 3y = 14 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 25 - 10y - 3y = 14 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 25 - 13y = 14 \\]\nSubtract 25 from both sides:\n\\[ -13y = 14 - 25 \\]\n\\[ -13y = -11 \\]\nDivide by -13:\n\\[ y = \\frac{11}{13} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 5 - 2y \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{11}{13} \\):\n\\[ x = 5 - 2\\left(\\frac{11}{13}\\right) \\]\nConvert 5 to a fraction with a denominator of 13:\n\\[ x = \\frac{65}{13} - \\frac{22}{13} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{43}{13} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{43}{13} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{11}{13} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 5x - 3y = 14 \\)\n\\[ 5\\left(\\frac{43}{13}\\right) - 3\\left(\\frac{11}{13}\\right) = 14 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{215}{13} - \\frac{33}{13} = 14 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{182}{13} = 14 \\]\n\\[ 14 = 14 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x + 4y = 10 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{43}{13}\\right) + 4\\left(\\frac{11}{13}\\right) = 10 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{86}{13} + \\frac{44}{13} = 10 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{130}{13} = 10 \\]\n\\[ 10 = 10 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{43}{13}, \\frac{11}{13}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 5x - 3y = 14 \\) and \\( 2x + 4y = 10 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{43}{13}, \\frac{11}{13}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 4x + 5y = 21 \\)\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 3x - 2y = 1 \\]\nAdd \\( 2y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ 3x = 1 + 2y \\]\nDivide by 3:\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 2y}{3} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 4x + 5y = 21 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{1 + 2y}{3} \\):\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{1 + 2y}{3}\\right) + 5y = 21 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ \\frac{4(1 + 2y)}{3} + 5y = 21 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{4 + 8y}{3} + 5y = 21 \\]\nMultiply through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 4 + 8y + 15y = 63 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 4 + 23y = 63 \\]\nSubtract 4 from both sides:\n\\[ 23y = 59 \\]\nDivide by 23:\n\\[ y = \\frac{59}{23} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 2y}{3} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{59}{23} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 2\\left(\\frac{59}{23}\\right)}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + \\frac{118}{23}}{3} \\]\nConvert 1 to a fraction with a denominator of 23:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{23}{23} + \\frac{118}{23}}{3} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{141}{23}}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{141}{23} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{141}{69} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{47}{23} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{47}{23} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{59}{23} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 4x + 5y = 21 \\)\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{47}{23}\\right) + 5\\left(\\frac{59}{23}\\right) = 21 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{188}{23} + \\frac{295}{23} = 21 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{483}{23} = 21 \\]\n\\[ 21 = 21 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{47}{23}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{59}{23}\\right) = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{141}{23} - \\frac{118}{23} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{23}{23} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{47}{23}, \\frac{59}{23}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 4x + 5y = 21 \\) and \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{47}{23}, \\frac{59}{23}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 3y = 11 \\)\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the first equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x + 3y = 11 \\]\nSubtract \\( 3y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 2x = 11 - 3y \\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 3y}{2} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 3x - 2y = 1 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{11 - 3y}{2} \\):\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{11 - 3y}{2}\\right) - 2y = 1 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ \\frac{3(11 - 3y)}{2} - 2y = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{33 - 9y}{2} - 2y = 1 \\]\nMultiply through by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 33 - 9y - 4y = 2 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 33 - 13y = 2 \\]\nSubtract 33 from both sides:\n\\[ -13y = 2 - 33 \\]\n\\[ -13y = -31 \\]\nDivide by -13:\n\\[ y = \\frac{31}{13} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 3y}{2} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{31}{13} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - 3\\left(\\frac{31}{13}\\right)}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 - \\frac{93}{13}}{2} \\]\nConvert 11 to a fraction with a denominator of 13:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{143}{13} - \\frac{93}{13}}{2} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{50}{13}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{50}{13} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{50}{26} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{25}{13} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{25}{13} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{31}{13} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 2x + 3y = 11 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{25}{13}\\right) + 3\\left(\\frac{31}{13}\\right) = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{50}{13} + \\frac{93}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{143}{13} = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{25}{13}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{31}{13}\\right) = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{75}{13} - \\frac{62}{13} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{13}{13} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{25}{13}, \\frac{31}{13}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 2x + 3y = 11 \\) and \\( 3x - 2y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{25}{13}, \\frac{31}{13}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 4y = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 2x - 3y = 1 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 2x - 3y = 1 \\]\nAdd \\( 3y \\) to both sides:\n\\[ 2x = 1 + 3y \\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 3y}{2} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 3x + 4y = 15 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{1 + 3y}{2} \\):\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{1 + 3y}{2}\\right) + 4y = 15 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ \\frac{3(1 + 3y)}{2} + 4y = 15 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{3 + 9y}{2} + 4y = 15 \\]\nMultiply through by 2 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 3 + 9y + 8y = 30 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 3 + 17y = 30 \\]\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\\[ 17y = 27 \\]\nDivide by 17:\n\\[ y = \\frac{27}{17} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 3y}{2} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = \\frac{27}{17} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + 3\\left(\\frac{27}{17}\\right)}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{1 + \\frac{81}{17}}{2} \\]\nConvert 1 to a fraction with a denominator of 17:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{17}{17} + \\frac{81}{17}}{2} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{98}{17}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{98}{17} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{98}{34} \\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ x = \\frac{49}{17} \\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\( x = \\frac{49}{17} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{27}{17} \\). We can verify this solution by substituting these values back into both original equations:\n1. \\( 3x + 4y = 15 \\)\n\\[ 3\\left(\\frac{49}{17}\\right) + 4\\left(\\frac{27}{17}\\right) = 15 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{147}{17} + \\frac{108}{17} = 15 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{255}{17} = 15 \\]\n\\[ 15 = 15 \\]\nThis is true.\n\n2. \\( 2x - 3y = 1 \\)\n\\[ 2\\left(\\frac{49}{17}\\right) - 3\\left(\\frac{27}{17}\\right) = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{98}{17} - \\frac{81}{17} = 1 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{17}{17} = 1 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 1 \\]\nThis is also true.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is \\( \\left(\\frac{49}{17}, \\frac{27}{17}\\right) \\).\n</think>.\n<answer>So, the solution to the system of equations \\( 3x + 4y = 15 \\) and \\( 2x - 3y = 1 \\) is \\( \\left(\\frac{49}{17}, \\frac{27}{17}\\right) \\).</answer> <|User|>Thank you for the detailed examples. They really helped me understand the substitution method. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to ensure I have a solid grasp of the method? <|Assistant|><think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider one more system of linear equations:\n1. \\( 4x - 5y = 11 \\)\n2. \\( 3x + 2y = 7 \\)\n\nStep 1: Solve one of the equations for one variable. Let's solve the second equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ 3x + 2y = 7 \\]\nSubtract \\( 2y \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 3x = 7 - 2y \\]\nDivide by 3:\n\\[ x = \\frac{7 - 2y}{3} \\]\n\nStep 2: Substitute the expression for \\( x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 4x - 5y = 11 \\]\nSubstitute \\( x = \\frac{7 - 2y}{3} \\):\n\\[ 4\\left(\\frac{7 - 2y}{3}\\right) - 5y = 11 \\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ \\frac{4(7 - 2y)}{3} - 5y = 11 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{28 - 8y}{3} - 5y = 11 \\]\nMultiply through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\\[ 28 - 8y - 15y = 33 \\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[ 28 - 23y = 33 \\]\nSubtract 28 from both sides:\n\\[ -23y = 5 \\]\nDivide by -23:\n\\[ y = -\\frac{5}{23} \\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\( y \\) back into the expression we found for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{7 - 2y}{3} \\]\nSubstitute \\( y = -\\frac{5}{23} \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{7 - 2\\left(-\\frac{5}{23}\\right)}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{7 + \\frac{10}{23}}{3} \\]\nConvert 7 to a fraction with a denominator of 23:\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\frac{161}{23} + \\frac{10}{23}}{3} \\]\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[ x