ed.\n\nTherefore, the product of 52 and 34 is \\(\\boxed{1768}\\).\n\nThis example should help further solidify your understanding of the area model method for multiplication. <|User|>This is all very helpful. Could you also provide an example using the standard algorithm for multiplying 87 and 41 to further solidify my understanding? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the problem of finding the product of 87 and 41 using the standard algorithm.\n\n1. **Write the numbers one below the other:**\n   ```\n   87\nx 41\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 87 by the units digit of 41 (which is 1):**\n   ```\n   87\nx  1\n---\n  87\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply 87 by the tens digit of 41 (which is 4), and remember to shift the result one place to the left:**\n   ```\n   87\nx 40\n---\n 3480\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the two results together:**\n   ```\n   87\nx 41\n---\n  87\n+3480\n---\n 3667\n   ```\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, we get the same result: 3667.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process written out:\n\n```\n  87\nx 41\n---\n  87  (87 * 1)\n+3480 (87 * 40, shifted one place to the left)\n---\n3667\n```\n\nTherefore, the product of 87 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{3667}\\).\n\nThis example should help further solidify your understanding of the standard algorithm for multiplication. <|User|>This is really helpful. Could you also provide an example using the lattice method for multiplying 68 and 53 to solidify my understanding? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! Let's provide an example using the lattice method to solve the multiplication of 68 and 53.\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid:**\n   - Draw a 2x2 grid because we are multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number.\n   - Write the digits of 68 along the top of the grid and the digits of 53 along the right side of the grid.\n\n```\n  6 | 8\n  -+--\n  5 | 3\n```\n\n2. **Divide each cell in the grid into two triangles:**\n   - Each cell will have a diagonal line dividing it into two parts.\n\n3. **Multiply the digits at the top and the right of each cell:**\n   - For the top-left cell (6 and 5):\n     ```\n     6 | 8\n     -+--\n     5 | 3\n     ```\n     Multiply 6 by 5 and write the result (30) in the top triangle.\n     Multiply 8 by 3 and write the result (24) in the bottom triangle.\n\n```\n  6 | 8\n  -+--\n  5 | 3\n 30 | 24\n```\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal, starting from the bottom right:**\n   - Add the numbers in the bottom right triangle (24) and the bottom left triangle (30):\n     ```\n     6 | 8\n     -+--\n     5 | 3\n     30 | 24\n     24\n     ```\n   - Add the numbers in the top right triangle (8) and the top left triangle (30):\n     ```\n     6 | 8\n     -+--\n     5 | 3\n     30 | 24\n     24\n     38\n     ```\n\n5. **Read the final product from the top left to the bottom right:**\n   - The final product is 3604.\n\nHere is the lattice grid with the final result:\n\n```\n  6 | 8\n  -+--\n  5 | 3\n 30 | 24\n 24 | 38\n 3604\n```\n\nTherefore, the product of 68 and 53 is \\(\\boxed{3604}\\).\n\nThis example should help further solidify your understanding of the lattice method for multiplication. <|User|>This is all very helpful. Could you also provide an example using the standard algorithm for multiplying 72 and 56 to further solidify my understanding? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the problem of finding the product of 72 and 56 using the standard algorithm.\n\n1. **Write the numbers one below the other:**\n   ```\n   72\nx 56\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 72 by the units digit of 56 (which is 6):**\n   ```\n   72\nx  6\n---\n 432\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply 72 by the tens digit of 56 (which is 5), and remember to shift the result one place to the left:**\n   ```\n   72\nx 50\n---\n 3600\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the two results together:**\n   ```\n   72\nx 56\n---\n 432\n+3600\n---\n 4032\n   ```\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, we get the same result: 4032.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process written out:\n\n```\n  72\nx 56\n-