      "text": "872.\n```.\n</answer>.<|User|>Okay, I understand the process. Can you show me how to solve it using the distributive property of multiplication over addition? Please reason step by step.<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! Using the distributive property of multiplication over addition, we can break down the multiplication of 64 by 13 into simpler parts. The distributive property states that \\(a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c\\). Here, we can express 13 as \\(10 + 3\\), and then apply the distributive property.\n..\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nUsing the distributive property, we can rewrite the multiplication as follows:\n\\[ 64 \\times 13 = 64 \\times (10 + 3) \\]\nBy the distributive property, this becomes:\n\\[ 64 \\times 13 = 64 \\times 10 + 64 \\times 3 \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate each part separately:\n1. Calculate \\(64 \\times 10\\):\n\\[ 64 \\times 10 = 640 \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(64 \\times 3\\):\n\\[ 64 \\times 3 = 192 \\]\n\nNow, add the two results together:\n\\[ 640 + 192 = 832 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 64 and 13 is:\n\\[ 64 \\times 13 = 832 \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{832} \\]\n</answer>",
