od that helps break down multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. For the multiplication of 19 and 87, we can represent 19 as a rectangle with dimensions 10 and 9, and 87 as a rectangle with dimensions 80 and 7. The area of the larger rectangle formed by combining these two smaller rectangles will give us the product of 19 and 87. Let's break it down step by step.\n..\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nTo solve 19 \u00d7 87 using the area model, we can break down the numbers into their tens and ones place values. We represent 19 as a 10 by 9 rectangle and 87 as an 80 by 7 rectangle. The area of the larger rectangle formed by combining these two smaller rectangles will give us the product of 19 and 87.\n\nFirst, let's draw the area model:\n\n```\n  10 |  9\n  ----|----\n  80 |  7\n```\n\nNow, we calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n1. The area of the 10 by 80 rectangle is:\n   10 \u00d7 80 = 800\n2. The area of the 9 by 80 rectangle is:\n   9 \u00d7 80 = 720\n3. The area of the 10 by 7 rectangle is:\n   10 \u00d7 7 = 70\n4. The area of the 9 by 7 rectangle is:\n   9 \u00d7 7 = 63\n\nNow, we add the areas of these smaller rectangles to find the total area:\n800 + 720 + 70 + 63 = 1653\n\nTherefore, using the area model, we find that 19 \u00d7 87 = 1653.\n</answer>",
