 rectangles:**\n   - One rectangle for 40 \u00d7 80\n   - One rectangle for 40 \u00d7 6\n   - One rectangle for 7 \u00d7 80\n   - One rectangle for 7 \u00d7 6\n\n4. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**\n   - 40 \u00d7 80 = 3200\n   - 40 \u00d7 6 = 240\n   - 7 \u00d7 80 = 560\n   - 7 \u00d7 6 = 42\n\n5. **Add the areas of the smaller rectangles together:**\n   - 3200 + 240 + 560 + 42 = 3200 + 240 + 560 + 42 = 3200 + 800 + 42 = 4000 + 42 = 4042\n\nSo, using the area model, we get:\n47 \u00d7 86 = 4042.\n\nThis method visually represents the multiplication and helps in understanding how each part contributes to the final product.\n</think>.\n<answer>\nUsing the area model, we can visualize the multiplication of 47 and 86 as follows:\n\n1. Break down 47 into 40 and 7, and 86 into 80 and 6.\n2. Draw a large rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles:\n   - One for 40 \u00d7 80\n   - One for 40 \u00d7 6\n   - One for 7 \u00d7 80\n   - One for 7 \u00d7 6\n\n3. Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n   - 40 \u00d7 80 = 3200\n   - 40 \u00d7 6 = 240\n   - 7 \u00d7 80 = 560\n   - 7 \u00d7 6 = 42\n\n4. Add the areas of the smaller rectangles together:\n   - 3200 + 240 + 560 + 42 = 3200 + 240 + 560 + 42 = 3200 + 800 + 42 = 4000 + 42 = 4042\n\nTherefore, using the area model, we find that:\n47 \u00d7 86 = 4042.\n</answer>",
