      "text": "638.</answer>.<|User|>The answer is correct. Here's another way to solve it: 11 * 58 = 11 * (60 - 2) = 11 * 60 - 11 * 2 = 660 - 22 = 638. This method is called the distributive property of multiplication over subtraction. Please explain the distributive property in more detail.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>\nThe distributive property of multiplication over addition or subtraction is a fundamental concept in arithmetic and algebra. It states that multiplying a number by a sum or difference is the same as multiplying the number by each term in the sum or difference and then adding or subtracting those products. Mathematically, it can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c \\]\n\\[ a \\times (b - c) = a \\times b - a \\times c \\]\n\nIn the given problem, \\( 11 \\times 58 \\), we can use the distributive property to break down the multiplication into simpler steps. The number 58 can be expressed as \\( 60 - 2 \\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[ 11 \\times 58 = 11 \\times (60 - 2) \\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[ 11 \\times (60 - 2) = 11 \\times 60 - 11 \\times 2 \\]\n\nNow, we can calculate each part separately:\n\n\\[ 11 \\times 60 = 660 \\]\n\\[ 11 \\times 2 = 22 \\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[ 660 - 22 = 638 \\]\n\nThis confirms that the distributive property allows us to simplify the multiplication by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable parts.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe distributive property of multiplication over subtraction is a powerful tool that simplifies the process of multiplication. In the given problem, \\( 11 \\times 58 \\), we used the distributive property by expressing 58 as \\( 60 - 2 \\). This allowed us to rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[ 11 \\times 58 = 11 \\times (60 - 2) = 11 \\times 60 - 11 \\times 2 \\]\n\nBy calculating each part separately and then subtracting, we arrived at the same result: \\( 660 - 22 = 638 \\). This method not only simplifies the multiplication but also helps in understanding the underlying structure of the numbers involved.\n</answer>",
